---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Shot from "../../components/Shot.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Learning Management System (LMS) Concept Demo"
  description="A working concept demonstration of a learning management system: applications, participants, cohorts, attendance, certificates and reporting. Built by Forthmark Systems with sample data."
  image="/images/work/programme-preview.png"
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="chip chip--concept">Concept demonstration</span>
      <div class="page-head__grid mt-2">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Concept demo / Training and education</p>
          <h1 class="h1">Learning Management System (LMS)</h1>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">
          A working demo for training providers and education teams: applications,
          participants, cohorts, attendance, certificate eligibility and reporting.
          Every record is sample data.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="fact-row">
        <div class="fact">
          <div class="fact__label">Type</div>
          <div class="fact__value">Concept demonstration</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <div class="fact__label">Data</div>
          <div class="fact__value">Sample, stored locally</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <div class="fact__label">Scope</div>
          <div class="fact__value">Applications, cohorts, attendance, certificates</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/demos/programme/">
          Open the working demo <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact">Discuss similar work</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="callout callout--concept">
        <p>
          <strong>This is not a client deployment.</strong> Aster Skills Academy is
          an invented training provider. The applicants, participants, cohorts,
          sessions and certificates are sample records and exist only to demonstrate how
          the platform behaves. No real learner or client data is used.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-3">
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/programme-preview.png"
          alt="Learning management system demo dashboard showing new applications, active participants, attendance, certificates ready, applications by track and upcoming sessions"
          caption="Dashboard: applications, active participants, attendance and certificate readiness from the same sample records."
          tag="Concept demo"
          wide
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="sticky-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
        <h2 class="h2">From application to certificate.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Training providers and education teams often run a complete
          journey across disconnected tools. Applications arrive by form, email and
          message. Enrolment lives in a spreadsheet. Attendance is a paper register.
          Certificates depend on someone reconstructing the records at the end.
        </p>
        <p>
          This demo connects the stages. An application is reviewed and accepted. An
          accepted applicant becomes a participant and is placed in a cohort with
          capacity. Sessions are scheduled. Attendance is marked against each
          session. Eligibility is calculated from recorded attendance and a
          configurable threshold. Certificates are issued and dated.
        </p>
        <p>
          The point is not the specific visual design. It is that one record follows
          the person through the programme, so each stage does not require a new
          spreadsheet.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper2">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="section-head__copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">What it covers</p>
          <h2 class="h2">The programme lifecycle.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Each view answers a question a programme manager actually asks during a
            live intake or cohort.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--3">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Applications</h3>
          <p>
            A review queue with source, track, date and status. Shortlist, accept or
            decline, and see the applicant's note before deciding.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Participants</h3>
          <p>
            Records for everyone enrolled, with track, cohort, status and attendance
            rate. Cohort and status can be changed from the record.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Cohorts</h3>
          <p>
            Capacity, dates and the full session schedule. Places left are visible,
            and new sessions can be added without leaving the view.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Attendance</h3>
          <p>
            Choose a cohort and session, then mark each participant present, absent
            or excused. Rates update immediately and the register exports as CSV.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Certificates</h3>
          <p>
            Eligibility is calculated from attendance and the configured threshold.
            The screen separates eligible, issued and not yet eligible participants.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Reports</h3>
          <p>
            Applications, acceptance rate, active participants, attendance and
            certificate counts, broken down by track and cohort.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="gallery mt-3">
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/programme-applications.png"
          alt="Applications view showing sample applicants, tracks, source, received date, status selectors and view buttons"
          caption="Applications: a review queue with status changes available in the list or in the applicant record."
          tag="Concept demo"
        />
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/programme-cohorts.png"
          alt="Cohorts view showing capacity meters, dates, session schedules and controls to add sessions or open attendance"
          caption="Cohorts: capacity, schedule and the controls that move work forward."
          tag="Concept demo"
        />
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/programme-attendance.png"
          alt="Attendance register for a sample cohort session with present, absent and excused options for each participant"
          caption="Attendance: one register per session, with current rates shown beside each participant."
          tag="Concept demo"
        />
        <Shot
          src="/images/work/programme-certificates.png"
          alt="Certificates view showing eligibility, attendance percentages, participant status and issue buttons"
          caption="Certificates: eligibility and issue dates, with the threshold visible on the screen."
          tag="Concept demo"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Interactions</p>
        <h2 class="h2">What actually works.</h2>
        <p class="lede mt-1">
          The demo behaves like software, not a slideshow. Actions change the stored
          records and the reports follow.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>Search and filter applications, participants and certificates.</li>
          <li>Shortlist, accept or decline an application.</li>
          <li>Accepting an applicant creates a participant record.</li>
          <li>Assign a participant to a cohort or change their status.</li>
          <li>Add a session to a cohort with a topic and date.</li>
          <li>Mark attendance and watch the rate and eligibility recalculate.</li>
          <li>Record a certificate issue date for an eligible participant.</li>
          <li>Export applications, participants, registers, certificates or a track report.</li>
          <li>Change the certificate threshold in settings and reset the demo.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout mt-2">
          <p>
            <strong>Data handling:</strong> records are stored in this browser using
            localStorage. Nothing is sent to a server, and clearing browser storage
            or resetting the demo removes your changes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper2">
    <div class="container split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Boundaries</p>
        <h2 class="h2">What this demo is not.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The demo covers the operational core. It deliberately leaves out the parts
          that require a real institution, real identity and real money.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>No login, roles or permissions. Anyone with the page can change the demo data.</li>
          <li>No shared database. Records do not sync between browsers or devices.</li>
          <li>No application form for the public. Applications are added inside the demo.</li>
          <li>No document upload, identity verification or assessment marking.</li>
          <li>No payment, invoicing or refund handling.</li>
          <li>No email or SMS notifications to applicants or participants.</li>
          <li>No certificate file generation. The demo records eligibility and an issue date only.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small">
          A real learning management system would need these features scoped against the
          institution's rules, not assumed from a demo.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">From demo to deployment</p>
        <h2 class="h2">How this maps to real work.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A production build starts with the programme rules: who can apply, how
          cohorts are formed, what counts as attendance, what makes someone eligible,
          who can issue a certificate, and what must be retained for audit.
        </p>
        <p>
          From there, the system gains authentication, a shared database, role based
          access, public application forms, notifications, document handling and
          reporting exports. The lifecycle demonstrated here stays the same, but each
          step is tested with the people who run the programme.
        </p>
        <div class="callout mt-2">
          <p>
            <strong>Technology note.</strong> The demo is built with Astro and
            vanilla JavaScript, with no framework runtime. State is stored locally
            and CSV exports are generated in the browser.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="btn-row mt-3">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/demos/programme/">
            Try the demo <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/work/operations-system">
            See the operations demo
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Running a programme on spreadsheets?"
    text="Describe the journey from application to completion. We will help you decide which parts need a system and which parts can stay simple."
    primaryLabel="Discuss a project"
    secondaryLabel="All work"
    secondaryHref="/work"
  />
</BaseLayout>
